1
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:39,600
CONVERSATION 1
Hi Lily, how's the new apartment?

2
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,960
It's okay.

3
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:41,880
What?

4
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:49,240
How can it be just okay when last week you were thrilled about the place and kept posting photos of it online?

5
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:55,360
Well, last week when I moved in, the apartment seemed cozy, just the right size for one person.

6
00:00:55,360 --> 00:01:01,260
But now it just seems tiny, shabby and solitary.

7
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:03,400
Ah that's the problem.

8
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,600
You miss your roommates from university, don't you?

9
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:22,700
I'm going to sound like an idiot because I used to complain to you all the time about how crowded our dormitory room was, and about all the things they did to irritate me, like watching movies late at night without headphones, or talking loudly early in the morning.

10
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:24,440
But now I miss them terribly.

11
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,520
Of course you do.

12
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,200
That's perfectly normal.

13
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:37,660
When I got my first place, I remember thinking I couldn't wait to live by myself and get away from my juvenile roommates and all their annoying habits.

14
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:44,420
But then I began missing them and feeling lonely and thinking that our dormitory was like paradise.

15
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:48,620
Even though there were six of us guys in one small room.

16
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:50,840
I thought it was just me who felt like that.

17
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:54,280
Look, you lived at home with us.

18
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,900
And then you had three roommates.

19
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,640
And this is your first time living alone.

20
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,080
So it's hard.

21
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:04,640
But your first apartment is a milestone in your life.

22
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,060
And you should celebrate it.

23
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:09,040
Tell me about the apartment.

24
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:11,100
Actually, it's not bad.

25
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,060
In fact, it's pretty adorable.

26
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,020
Now that I have decorated it and it has everything I need.

27
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:20,120
I have a kitchen to cook in, the bathroom all to myself.

28
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:27,680
And then another room with my bed at one end and the sofa, a small table and chairs at the other end.

29
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,720
That does sound adorable, and I can't wait to see it.

30
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:40,040
And neither can mom and dad.

31
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:58,660
Q1. What was the woman's first impression of the apartment?

32
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:17,320
Q2. Why does the woman say she's going to sound like an idiot?

33
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:37,160
Q3. What do we learn about the man when he left the dormitory to live on his own?

34
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:55,300
Q4. What is the man say he can't wait to do?

35
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:59,040
CONVERSATION 2
Welcome to our program book talk.

36
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:09,400
Our guest today is Frank Jones, a critic of our education system and the author of new book, How to reform our universities.

37
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,000
Hello, Susan.

38
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,980
Frank, you support radically changing universities in America.

39
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,360
Yes.

40
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:26,980
I believe that the purpose of higher education is to prepare young people to enter the workforce and that our current system fails to do this.

41
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:32,860
We're allocating too many resources to disciplines that don't match the needs of employers.

42
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:36,280
I think your attitude to education is a bit cynical Frank.

43
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:47,140
Surely the purpose of university is to prepare young people to participate fully in civic life rather than just to find well paid jobs.

44
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:53,320
Susan, many young graduates struggle to find any job let alone a good one.

45
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:55,220
The job market is grim.

46
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:57,900
Particularly for students who study the arts.

47
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:09,260
I agree that it isn't easy for young people to find work, but you propose closing down all departments that aren't directly related to science and technology.

48
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:11,380
Is that really the solution?

49
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:13,540
You're overstating my point.

50
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:20,160
My argument is that we need to use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering.

51
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:25,420
To do that, we need to take money from subjects like literature and music.

52
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:27,400
But the arts have value.

53
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:29,700
They're an important part of our culture.

54
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:35,120
Studying literature or music or sculpture might not result in a job in that area.

55
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:44,060
But it helps young people to think about the world in a deeper way, which makes them better citizens and makes for a better society.

56
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:54,960
I agree that the arts are valuable to society, but it's naive to think that not only the most talented, but all students should study them at university level.

57
00:05:54,960 --> 00:06:16,705
The odds are very competitive, and most graduates will end up with a great deal of debt, obtaining a degree that has little value on the job market.

58
00:06:16,705 --> 00:06:31,460
Q5. What do we learn from the conversation about the man?

59
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:51,820
Q6. What does the man believe is the problem with the current American System of Higher Education?

60
00:06:51,820 --> 00:07:10,100
Q7. How should the education budget be allocated according to the man?

61
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:55,000
Q8. What does the woman say the arts can do?

62
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,880
PASSAGE 1
Do you ever have the annoying feeling that you don't have time to really think anymore?

63
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:02,220
You're not alone.

64
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:10,920
A variety of factors have conspired to rob us of time for reflection on ourselves and our lives.

65
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:14,400
Preoccupied minds are rarely silent.

66
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:19,580
The average person receives hundreds of texts and voice messages a day.

67
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:29,860
And holidays for many of us are action-packed weeks more likely full of family activities than opportunities for tranquility and contemplation.

68
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:35,040
Regular reflection, however, underlies all great professionals.

69
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:43,820
It's a prerequisite for you to recharge your mental batteries, see things in a new light and tap into your creativity.

70
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:52,980
Almost all of the great advisors that I have studied have found ways to get away from it all and contemplate their life and work.

71
00:08:52,980 --> 00:09:04,880
Some researchers in the field of creativity, in fact, believe that insight occurs during the reflection and relaxation that follows a period of intense activity.

72
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:10,540
Schedule your time for reflection about your work or a particular project you're engaged in.

73
00:09:10,540 --> 00:09:13,520
I usually block out half an hour.

74
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,300
Don't answer the phone.

75
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:17,880
Push your papers to the side.

76
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:22,840
Sketch, make lists, draw mind maps of ideas that come to you.

77
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:27,700
At the end, write down any emerging ideas.

78
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:31,500
When you're alone, stop worrying and think.

79
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:40,500
A lot of our downtime is spent worrying about troubles or things in our lives or fantasizing about how we'd like our lives to be.

80
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:46,340
Revisit things during moments of relaxation after a period of intense work.

81
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:56,340
This is when we are the most creative.

82
00:09:56,340 --> 00:10:19,716
Q9. What do we learn about the feeling that one doesn't have time to think anymore?

83
00:10:19,716 --> 00:10:35,300
Q10. What trait do all great professionals share?

84
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:55,700
Q11. What do some researchers believe is conducive to creative ideas?

85
00:10:55,700 --> 00:11:00,270
PASSAGE 2
Before Colorado became a state, it already had post offices.

86
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:08,030
The first opened in 1859 in a settlement founded by migrants searching for gold.

87
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:10,870
Life could be unpredictable out west.

88
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:13,550
Gold failed to appear.

89
00:11:13,550 --> 00:11:18,790
Drought ruined farmers, and settlers clashed with Native Americans.

90
00:11:18,790 --> 00:11:24,750
On the settlement's location now stands a sprawling University campus.

91
00:11:24,750 --> 00:11:31,190
Amid all the changes, one feature remained constant: the postal service.

92
00:11:31,190 --> 00:11:36,150
The maps tracing America's westward expansion are telling.

93
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:47,330
In 1864 there were few postal branches on land controlled by Native Americans, which still accounted for most of the West.

94
00:11:47,330 --> 00:11:52,730
Over the next 25 years, post offices grew quickly.

95
00:11:52,730 --> 00:12:00,430
Colonization of the West could be regarded as a result of big government rather than pioneers.

96
00:12:00,430 --> 00:12:08,850
As federal subsidies and land grants tempted people into the deserts and plains, the post kept them connected.

97
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:17,590
In the mid-19th century, the Post Office Department was far from a centralized bureaucracy.

98
00:12:17,590 --> 00:12:25,130
To keep up with migration patterns, postal services were added to existing businesses.

99
00:12:25,130 --> 00:12:30,210
The federal government commissioned private wagons to carry the mail.

100
00:12:30,210 --> 00:12:35,310
Short term contracts were granted to local businessmen to act as postmasters.

101
00:12:35,310 --> 00:12:45,370
These partnerships enabled the mail to quickly follow migrants, helping knit together remote parts of the country.

102
00:12:45,370 --> 00:12:52,410
Mr. Bellavance, a digital historian, wrote a book on the history of the US postal service.

103
00:12:52,410 --> 00:12:57,550
He used data science to analyze historical trends.

104
00:12:57,550 --> 00:13:03,710
Most strikingly he built an accompanying website, complete with interactive maps.

105
00:13:03,710 --> 00:13:12,770
They show readers how within a generation the postal service helped colonize a continent.

106
00:13:12,770 --> 00:13:26,190
These online interactive maps illustrate the formative power of snail mail.

107
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:45,570
Q12. What does the passage say about Colorado before it became a state?

108
00:13:45,570 --> 00:14:05,550
Q13. How did the postal service contribute to America's westward expansion?

109
00:14:05,550 --> 00:14:26,790
Q14. What did the federal government do to meet the increasing demand for the postal service in the West?

110
00:14:26,790 --> 00:15:13,030
Q15. What did Mr. Bellavance do to study the history of the US postal service?

111
00:15:13,030 --> 00:15:18,630
RECORDING 1
In last week's lecture, we discussed reasons why people forget things.

112
00:15:18,630 --> 00:15:25,750
This week we will discuss a surprising reason why we might remember some things: anxiety.

113
00:15:25,750 --> 00:15:28,910
Think about something as simple as buying a coffee.

114
00:15:28,910 --> 00:15:33,610
That may not seem like an experience that would make a deep impression on your memory.

115
00:15:33,610 --> 00:15:36,770
But anxiety could change that.

116
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:48,430
In fact, a new study suggests that people with higher anxiety levels might remember certain information better than people with lower anxiety levels.

117
00:15:48,430 --> 00:15:58,510
That's because higher levels of anxiety may make people more susceptible to negative feelings, putting them in a more negative state of mind.

118
00:15:58,510 --> 00:16:03,750
That in turn, may make them able to better remember some events.

119
00:16:03,750 --> 00:16:07,170
Let's take a closer look at that new study now.

120
00:16:07,170 --> 00:16:14,010
In this study, the researchers started by giving 80 undergraduate students an anxiety test.

121
00:16:14,010 --> 00:16:19,770
The test measured the participants' anxiety levels over the proceeding two weeks.

122
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:27,130
Then, to test memory, the participants were shown a series of neutral words one at a time.

123
00:16:27,130 --> 00:16:39,290
Some of the words were printed onto photos of negative scenes, meaning images that could affect their emotions negatively, such as a photo of a car accident, or a cemetery.

124
00:16:39,290 --> 00:16:46,790
The rest of the words were printed onto photos of neutral scenes, such as a photo of a lake or trees.

125
00:16:46,790 --> 00:16:54,430
Neutral words included words like table or desk that don't elicit emotion.

126
00:16:54,430 --> 00:17:04,330
Later, the participants were asked to think back to the words they were shown earlier, which caused them to reenter either a negative or neutral mindset.

127
00:17:04,330 --> 00:17:11,330
The participants were then presented with another set of neutral words, and their memory of these new words was tested.

128
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:24,770
The researchers found that the new words presented to people in a negative mindset were better remembered by people with higher levels of anxiety than those with lower levels of anxiety.

129
00:17:24,770 --> 00:17:38,250
In other words, when highly anxious individuals took in otherwise emotionally neutral information that was presented to them, it became colored by their negative mindset, making them remember the information better.

130
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:44,670
But these same effects were not seen in people with low levels of anxiety.

131
00:17:44,670 --> 00:17:57,730
Previous studies have found that extreme levels of anxiety such as those experienced by people diagnosed with an anxiety disorder can be quite detrimental to memory and cognitive performance.

132
00:17:57,730 --> 00:18:14,830
But the highly anxious people in this study represent individuals who are managing their anxiety and for whom anxiety is not a serious problem.

133
00:18:14,830 --> 00:18:33,490
Q16. What does the speaker say the new study suggests?

134
00:18:33,490 --> 00:18:51,600
Q17. What did researchers do first in the new study?

135
00:18:51,600 --> 00:19:09,710
Q18. What do we learn from previous studies about anxiety?

136
00:19:09,710 --> 00:19:17,110
RECORDING 2
Over the past 20 years, the internet has gradually become a dominant feature of our lives.

137
00:19:17,110 --> 00:19:19,490
It has changed how we communicate with each other.

138
00:19:19,490 --> 00:19:24,710
And it has definitely transformed the way we do business with each other.

139
00:19:24,710 --> 00:19:28,550
Marketing has also changed in a number of ways.

140
00:19:28,550 --> 00:19:37,990
For instance, in the past, consumers had to call a phone number and patiently wait on hold in order to get the information they wanted.

141
00:19:37,990 --> 00:19:41,170
Today, they want the information immediately.

142
00:19:41,170 --> 00:19:49,390
They'll go to the company's social media pages and post comments and questions expecting to receive an immediate response.

143
00:19:49,390 --> 00:19:57,670
If they don't get their questions answered soon, they'll move on to another company that will answer them quickly.

144
00:19:57,670 --> 00:20:02,190
Marketing departments today need to follow technological development.

145
00:20:02,190 --> 00:20:07,370
For example, this year's smartphone is smarter than last year's.

146
00:20:07,370 --> 00:20:10,330
Self-driving cars are now on the road.

147
00:20:10,330 --> 00:20:20,830
Marketers have to do research on which technologies are coming into being, otherwise, they risk being left behind in the virtual dust.

148
00:20:20,830 --> 00:20:25,270
Marketing has also changed due to the importance of video.

149
00:20:25,270 --> 00:20:28,130
People don't just want to read text.

150
00:20:28,130 --> 00:20:30,850
They want to watch things happening.

151
00:20:30,850 --> 00:20:39,390
Companies now have to explore how they can use video on a consistent basis to share information about their businesses.

152
00:20:39,390 --> 00:20:44,470
Fortunately, it's extremely easy to shoot something these days.

153
00:20:44,470 --> 00:20:47,410
All you need is a smartphone.

154
00:20:47,410 --> 00:20:50,530
But what's the result of all this?

155
00:20:50,530 --> 00:20:52,570
Shorter attention spans?

156
00:20:52,570 --> 00:20:57,070
We aren't the same people that we were 20 years ago.

157
00:20:57,070 --> 00:21:05,790
Not only have we grown accustomed to getting the information we want instantaneously, our attention spans are much shorter.

158
00:21:05,790 --> 00:21:13,930
If something doesn't capture our attention within a few seconds, we're on to the next piece of content.

159
00:21:13,930 --> 00:21:25,270
Marketers need to figure out ways to speak directly to the customer's emotions and they need to figure out how to do that as quickly as possible.

160
00:21:25,270 --> 00:21:28,710
Once people are emotionally engaged, they'll stick with you.

161
00:21:28,710 --> 00:21:37,650
If marketing has changed this much in the past 20 years, imagine what the next 20 years will bring.

162
00:21:37,650 --> 00:21:47,150
In a recent survey, only 9% of marketers could say with confidence that their marketing efforts were actually working.

163
00:21:47,150 --> 00:21:53,650
Their confidence is being shaken because the rules of the game change every year.

164
00:21:53,650 --> 00:22:04,510
That's why it's important for marketers to pay attention to the latest technological developments and consider collaborating with technological innovators.

165
00:22:04,510 --> 00:22:17,210
That way, they'll be moving at the same pace as the tech industry.

166
00:22:17,210 --> 00:22:34,990
Q19. What does the speaker say about today's consumers?

167
00:22:34,990 --> 00:22:54,730
Q20. How do marketers capture consumers' attention as quickly as possible?

168
00:22:54,730 --> 00:23:14,970
Q21. What does the speaker suggest marketers do to meet future challenges?

169
00:23:14,970 --> 00:23:34,010
RECORDING 3
You might be surprised to learn that the benefits of friendships extend beyond people's social life and into their work, which is interesting when you consider the extent to which people sacrifice friendships, or at least the time they spend with friends because of the extra hours they're devoting to work.

170
00:23:34,010 --> 00:23:40,930
Just last week, I was remarking to a colleague that I'm content with only one social engagement per week.

171
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:46,450
But according to recent research, that's evidently not enough.

172
00:23:46,450 --> 00:23:59,790
In an initial study of more than 700 respondents, scholars from an American university analyzed the impact that friends, as opposed to family, have on self-esteem and well-being.

173
00:23:59,790 --> 00:24:02,990
Friends came out substantially on top.

174
00:24:02,990 --> 00:24:06,850
That's because to be someone's mate is a voluntary act.

175
00:24:06,850 --> 00:24:10,390
Unlike family who people rarely get to choose.

176
00:24:10,390 --> 00:24:21,860
The researchers found that when people choose to cultivate and maintain supportive friendships with an individual, it means that the person is valued and worthy of their limited time.

177
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:27,180
Such sentiments of value and worthiness boost our self-esteem.

178
00:24:27,180 --> 00:24:31,660
The second study comprised more than 300 participants.

179
00:24:31,660 --> 00:24:39,380
It proved that the better we feel about ourselves, the more likely we will perform our job confidently and competently.

180
00:24:39,380 --> 00:24:45,160
This follow-up study found that non-work friends even improved people's job satisfaction.

181
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:56,180
They have as much of an impact on how much they love their jobs, as do the friends they have at work, despite not actually being at our place of work.

182
00:24:56,180 --> 00:25:02,980
These types of friends tend to be our preferred outlet for complaining about work-related matters.

183
00:25:02,980 --> 00:25:07,380
This is an avenue that may not be available at the office.

184
00:25:07,380 --> 00:25:20,600
So even though friendships can be easy to neglect when confronted by pressures at work, or even pressures at home, neglecting our friends can turn out to be harmful and counterproductive.

185
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:34,640
That's why when determining how to create a better work-life balance, we need to consider not only how to balance work and family demands, but also how to cultivate and sustain supportive friendships.

186
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:44,200
It's for this reason that managers should never discriminate when it comes to requests from employees for flexible work arrangements

187
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:54,340
It's irrelevant whether their need for a desired schedule is due to say, parenting responsibilities, or a craving to hang out with their best mate.

188
00:25:54,340 --> 00:25:59,580
What matters is the opportunity to engage in a nourishing activity outside of work.

189
00:25:59,580 --> 00:26:12,520
That will definitely have a follow-on effect at work.

190
00:26:12,520 --> 00:26:30,920
Q22. What does the speaker say is interesting?

191
00:26:30,920 --> 00:26:51,860
Q23. What did researchers from an American university analyze in their initial study?

192
00:26:51,860 --> 00:27:12,140
Q24. What did the second study find about non-work friends?

193
00:27:12,140 --> 00:27:32,715
Q25. What does the speaker suggest managers do?
